+# Register localization (--local-regs)
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Every lifted statement reads and writes the guest registers in ctx->gpr[], and
+# the lifted TUs build with -fno-strict-aliasing: each local-store write (a store
+# through a may_alias pointer) forces the compiler to reload every register it
+# is holding, and each value then reaches its next use through memory, a
+# store-to-load forwarding round trip on the critical path. With --local-regs, a
+# function's registers live in locals between the points where something else
+# can look at ctx: a register is written back before and reloaded after any
+# statement that passes ctx to anything but the local-store accessors, and
+# written back before every exit. The statements that do cross that line run
+# unchanged against ctx->gpr, so they need no knowledge of what each callee
+# reads or writes. (Ported from the MotorStorm Apocalypse port's lifter and
+# adapted to this lifter's SPU_DIRECT exits and merged-chunk interrupt checks.)
